From 0190f3467d17a500be2168658a4284a22364566e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 05:30:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Snapshot config and memoize run effects per blocker
 pass

The hub P1 after PR #2915 measured the shared-blocker lanes at 1.383% of a
core. Profiling them on a copy of the hub board with hub's own config showed
normalize_blocker_id doing a cache-hit load_config_readonly (~31 us on the
hub) for every run row: ~1,500 loads per blocker_states call.

- kanban_blocker_inputs.config(): one config snapshot per input pass. A
  validated input read whose live aliases differ from the snapshot's alias
  copy replaces it (in-place edits included) and drops every memo. Failed
  loads are never retained; outside a pass every call reads fresh.
- run_effect memoizes its decoded result per metadata string inside a pass
  (pure in the row and the snapshot).
- sweep_shared_blockers runs its three lanes inside one input pass, so they
  share the snapshot, the memo and the validated inputs. Writes, peer
  commits and alias changes still invalidate; no BlockerState is shared.

RED: blocker_states over 12 assertions loaded config 40 times (now <= 6);
sweep lanes had no shared scope. Mutant (memo not dropped on alias change)
fails two alias tests. GREEN: 228 tests across 17 blocker/sweep-gate files.

Hub-copy replay on the hub with its installed venv and config, 15 runs,
median CPU: blocker_states 27.6 -> 10.5 ms; check/release/probe lanes
39.8/27.1/27.5 -> 11.3/10.5/11.0 ms; all three lanes in one sweep 94.7 ->
30.1 ms. This is a replay, not the live P1. The whole reclaim phase is still
over its 0.4% budget until a live P1 shows otherwise.
